 "api_error_status": null,
 "result": "**Where to look**\n\n- `src/tessel/app.py:1184-1190` in `Tessel.link_for`. This is the `RuntimeError` you're seeing. With no request context, it calls `self.build_url_binder(None)` and raises if that returns `None`.\n- `src/tessel/app.py:550-558` in `Tessel.build_url_binder`. With no request, it only returns an adapter when `config[\"SERVER_NAME\"]` is not `None`. Otherwise it returns `None`, which triggers the error above.\n\nYour background job has no request, so `SERVER_NAME` is unset and the binder comes back `None`.\n\n**Fix (config)**\n\n```python\napp.config[\"SERVER_NAME\"] = \"example.com\"          # required; add :port if non-default\napp.config[\"APPLICATION_ROOT\"] = \"/\"               # set if the app is mounted under a prefix\napp.config[\"PREFERRED_URL_SCHEME\"] = \"https\"       # defaults to \"http\"\n```\n\n`SERVER_NAME` is the one that actually fixes the crash. The other two control the path prefix and scheme of the generated URLs.\n\nOutside a request, `link_for` produces external URLs by default (`_external=True`, line 1194). Call it as `app.link_for(...)` directly; `tessel.link_for` expects an active app context.\n\nAlternatively, run the job inside `with app.test_request_context():`, which supplies a request-bound adapter (see `docs/appcontext.rst`). Setting `SERVER_NAME` is the cleaner option for a real background job.",
